<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nonantola Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/752">752</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nonantola Abbey is a Benedictine monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded in 752 by Anselm, a former Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> and brother-in-law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a>. Favoured by Lombard and Carolingian rulers, it grew into one of the richest abbeys of Italy, with a well-known scriptorium. Its Romanesque church dates from the 12th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nonantola-abbey.jpg" alt="Nonantola Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbazia_di_nonantola_00.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The abbey stands in the commune of Nonantola, about 10 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> in the Emilia Romagna region. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> endowed it richly, making it one of the main landowners of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Pope Stephen II appointed Anselm its first abbot and gave it relics of Saint Sylvester, to whom it was dedicated as S. Silvestro de Nonantula. After Aistulf's death in 756 the new king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a>, banished Anselm to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> restored him seven years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its abbots served the emperors: Peter of Nonantola was imperial ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 813, and his successor Ansfrid held the same post in 828. In 883 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> and Pope Marinus I met there. In 900 invading Hungarians destroyed the monastery and church and killed everyone who had not fled, but rebuilding began almost at once. Until the 11th century it was an imperial monastery, and emperors and popes contended for control of its abbatial elections.</p><p>In the Investiture Conflict it first sided with the emperor, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a> forced it to submit to the pope in 1083, and in 1111 it declared openly for the papal party; that year the monk Placidus of Nonantola wrote De honore Ecclesiæ, an important defence of the papal position. Decline began in 1419 under commendatory abbots, among them Giuliano Della Rovere and Carlo Borromeo. Pope Clement XIII suppressed the abbey in 1768, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> restored it as a monastery on 23 January 1821, and the Italian government appropriated the buildings in 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The church, built under Abbot Damian and restored in the early 20th century to its early 12th-century form, has a nave, two aisles and a crypt of sixty-four columns dating from the 8th century. The crypt holds the relics of Saint Anselm, who died on 3 March 803, of Saint Sylvester, Saint Adrian III and other saints. The church is a basilica and the co-cathedral of the Diocese of Modena-Nonantola, formed in 1986. The surviving buildings house the town hall, where 11th-century frescoes were found, a museum of sacred art and the abbey's archives and library.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbey of Nonantola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Carolingians kept Lombard law and the palace at Pavia but installed Frankish counts and bishops, issued capitularies for Italy and supported monasteries such as Bobbio and <b>Nonantola</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/carolingian-kingdom-of-italy">Carolingian Kingdom of Italy</a></li></ul></section>
